UPDATE companies SET description='Buitoni Food Company, headquartered in Danville, Virginia, traces its origins to 1827 in Sansepolcro, Tuscany, where founder Giulia Buitoni traded a family necklace for a pasta machine and sourced wheat from Puglia after a 40-day journey. The company’s early growth included building a factory that earned recognition from the King of Italy after a 1905 fire, and by 1901 it had received the Italian Labour Merit Medal. Giovanni Buitoni brought the brand to New York City in 1939, establishing a U.S. presence that continues today.

The company manufactures fresh pasta, sauces, and cheeses at its Danville facility, emphasizing artisan techniques such as slow-roasting chicken, importing Italian 00 flour, and using aged DOP Parmigiano Reggiano and farm-fresh eggs.

Buitoni serves the retail grocery and foodservice sectors, distributing products through select retailers nationwide. Its customer base includes supermarket chains and consumers seeking authentic Italian meal solutions, with recipes and product locators provided on its website.

The brand differentiates itself through a commitment to Tuscan craftsmanship and ingredient integrity, rooted in Giulia Buitoni’s original pursuit of the finest wheat. The company states that “details matter” in every step, from flour milling to cheese selection, aiming to deliver a modern Italian masterpiece in each product.', certifications=ARRAY[]::text[], profile='{"capabilities": ["Fresh pasta production", "Sauce manufacturing", "Cheese processing", "Artisan pasta techniques", "Slow-roasting", "Ingredient sourcing (imported flour, DOP cheese)"], "industries": ["Food manufacturing", "Retail grocery", "Foodservice", "Consumer packaged goods"], "materials": ["Imported 00 flour", "Aged DOP Parmigiano Reggiano", "Farm-fresh eggs", "Wheat from Puglia", "Water"], "equipment": [], "volume": "", "lead_time": "", "tolerances": "", "built_on": ["Imported Italian 00 flour", "Aged DOP Parmigiano Reggiano", "Farm-fresh eggs", "Slow-roasted chicken", "Artisan pasta-making techniques"], "facts_used": ["Founded 1827 in Sansepolcro, Tuscany by Giulia Buitoni -> /about", "Headquarters in Danville, Virginia -> db", "Manufactures fresh pasta, sauces, and cheeses -> /", "Uses imported Italian 00 flour, aged DOP Parmigiano Reggiano, farm-fresh eggs -> /", "Factory burned in 1905, King of Italy donated 1,500 lire -> /about", "Received Italian Labour Merit Medal in 1901 -> /about", "Giovanni Buitoni opened first U.S. store in New York City in 1939 -> /about", "Products sold at select retailers nationwide -> /"], "company_type": "Manufacturer", "makes_products": "yes", "type_evidence": "Buitoni Pasta, Sauces & Cheese", "founded_year": "1827", "employee_count": "", "cage_code": "", "min_order": "", "export_compliance": [], "languages": [], "social_links": [], "ownership_tags": []}'::jsonb, updated_at=now() WHERE slug='buitoni-food-company' AND approved AND profile IS NULL;
UPDATE companies SET description='Bullock Breakers is a family-owned business based in Annandale, Minnesota, that opened in May 2015. The company was founded to supply used air and vacuum circuit breakers, switchgear, and related components to customers seeking reliable alternatives to new equipment.

The company maintains an inventory of thousands of used air and vacuum circuit breakers from major brands, along with switchgear, parts breakers, ground and test units, and PT/VT drawers. In addition to sales, Bullock Breakers operates a service shop offering reconditioning—complete teardown, rebuild, lubrication, and full testing—retrofit of trip units (including AC-PRO II, MicroVersa Trip Plus/PM, Entelliguard, and Digitrip models), and repair services that include visual inspection, testing, and troubleshooting.

Bullock Breakers serves the electric power systems sector, providing components critical for conducting or interrupting power in switchgear applications. Its customers include maintenance and operations teams that need to de-energize or isolate equipment, as well as organizations looking to reduce costs and lead times compared to purchasing new gear.

